---
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import meta from "../data/meta.json";
import methodology from "../data/methodology.json";
import { Content as MethodologyText } from "../content/methodology.md";

const rubricModules = import.meta.glob("../data/rubrics/*/*.md", {
  eager: true,
  query: "?raw",
  import: "default",
});
const rubrics: { axis: string; category: string; text: string }[] = Object.entries(
  rubricModules
)
  .map(([path, text]) => {
    const m = path.match(/rubrics\/([^/]+)\/([^/]+)\.md$/)!;
    return { axis: m[1], category: m[2], text: text as string };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => (a.axis + a.category).localeCompare(b.axis + b.category));

const displayNames: Record<string, string> = Object.fromEntries(
  meta.axes.map((a: any) => [a.axis_id, a.display_name])
);
const spearman: Record<string, Record<string, Record<string, number | null>>> =
  methodology.spearman_per_axis;
---

<Base title="Methodology" active="methodology" heading="How Rancor measures this">
  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <p class="note">
    Bootstrap: B = {methodology.bootstrap_b} resamples over items, percentile
    95% intervals, seeded. This run: {methodology.disagreement_flagged}
    judge-disagreement flags, {methodology.human_overridden} human overrides.
  </p>

  <h2>What this preview run actually did</h2>
  <p class="note">
    The methodology below describes the full protocol. This preview ran a
    reduced configuration, and the difference matters when reading the
    numbers:
  </p>
  <ul class="note">
    <li>
      <strong>Conditions</strong>: base condition only. The chain-of-thought
      and persona variants and the temperature-0.7 robustness slice were not
      run, which is why model pages report CoT amplification and persona
      effect as “not run”.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Groups</strong>: counterfactual items ran the focal group and
      the first comparison group only, not the full comparison set.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Human review</strong>: {methodology.disagreement_flagged} records
      were flagged for judge disagreement and
      {methodology.human_overridden === 0
        ? ` none have been adjudicated by a human. All ${methodology.disagreement_flagged} have been read by an agent (${methodology.agent_adjudicated} graded, ${methodology.adjudication_escalated} escalated as rubric gaps), recorded in a separate file that the scoring pipeline never reads, so every published score in this preview is still a judge median, unchanged. The review queue and both adjudication files ship with the run for anyone to check.`
        : ` ${methodology.human_overridden} carry a human grade in place of the median.`}
    </li>
  </ul>

  <MethodologyText />

  <h2>Inter-category correlation (Spearman, per axis)</h2>
  <p class="note">
    Correlations are computed across models, so this preview's matrix rests
    on five points per pair: enough to compute a coefficient, not enough to
    interpret one. Values near ±1 (including exact 1.00) and empty cells are
    expected at this sample size; the question of whether the categories
    measure non-redundant behavior is one the full run answers, not this
    table. Cells read "—" where the correlation is undefined: either fewer
    than three scored models, or a category on which every model scored
    identically (a constant has no rank correlation).
  </p>
  {
    Object.entries(spearman).map(([axis, matrix]) => {
      const categories = Object.keys(matrix);
      return (
        <section>
          <h3>{displayNames[axis] ?? axis}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ledger matrix">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th />
                {categories.map((c) => (
                  <th class="num">{c.slice(0, 12)}</th>
                ))}
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {categories.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><Term t={row.replaceAll("_", " ").replace(/s$/, "")}>{row.replaceAll("_", " ")}</Term></td>
                  {categories.map((col) => (
                    <td class="num">
                      {matrix[row][col] === null ? "—" : matrix[row][col]!.toFixed(2)}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
        </section>
      );
    })
  }

  <h2>The standard we hold this to</h2>

  <p>
    We did not invent the bar. In its analysis of another organisation's
    hate-incident data, the Muslim Public Affairs Council set out what a
    dataset in this space owes the people who rely on it: be clear how the
    conclusions were derived, release the underlying records, and tag them
    consistently, or take the numbers down until you can. Here is this
    instrument answered against them, item by item.
  </p>

  <dl class="standard">
    <dt>Be clear how the conclusion was derived</dt>
    <dd>
      Every rubric that produced a score is printed in full below, the same
      text the judges were given. The metric definitions, the interval method
      and the tie rule are on this page. Nothing is computed by a step that is
      not written down.
    </dd>
    <dt>Release the underlying records</dt>
    <dd>
      Every model answer and all three judge rationales behind every published
      number are readable in the transcript browser, not summarised. The frozen
      prompt set, the scored records and the run manifest are in the
      repository. There is no private tier.
    </dd>
    <dt>Tag consistently, or withdraw the number</dt>
    <dd>
      One prompt set, hashed; one rubric per category, applied to every model
      and every group identically. Where the judges disagreed by more than a
      point the record is flagged and queued rather than averaged quietly.
      Where the intervals overlap the table shares the rank instead of printing
      an order the data cannot support, which is the withdrawal half of the
      rule, applied to ourselves.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="note">
    Reference: MPAC, Center for Security, Technology and Policy, 2025
    data-analysis report, closing recommendations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before-it-ships">Test a model before it ships</h2>
  <p>
    The same instrument runs as an MCP server, so a team can grade a candidate
    model against the same prompts, rubrics and judge panel before release;
    the quickstart, the six tools and the builder loop are on
    <a href="/mcp/">the builders page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Rubrics</h2>
  {
    rubrics.length === 0 ? (
      <div class="empty-state">
        Rubric files are drafted and awaiting human review in
        <code> prompts/_candidates/</code>; they render here once promoted to
        <code> prompts/v1.0/</code>.
      </div>
    ) : (
      rubrics.map((rubric) => (
        <details class="rubric-block">
          <summary>
            {displayNames[rubric.axis] ?? rubric.axis} ·{" "}
            <Term t={rubric.category.replaceAll("_", " ").replace(/s$/, "")}>{rubric.category.replaceAll("_", " ")}</Term>
          </summary>
          <pre>{rubric.text}</pre>
        </details>
      ))
    )
  }
</Base>
